Stanisław Tymiński was a Canadian-Peruvian entrepreneur born in Poland who was the sitting leader of the Libertarian Party of Canada.<ref name="WaPo1990">{{cite news |last = Battiata |first = Mary |title = Polish Government Links Tyminski to Libya, Secret Police |newspaper = The Washington Post |date = 30 November 1990 |url = https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/politics/1990/12/01/polish-government-links-tyminski-to-libya-secret-police/3fd661c1-2981-4d37-aabc-bab5b3ebc25f/ |quote = "With little more than a week until the election, the Polish-born Tymiński, leader of the small, right-wing Libertarian Party of Canada, remains a shadowy figure."}}</ref> He returned to Poland to contest the presidential elections. He was the only candidate without any political party's backing who managed to cross the threshold of 100,000 signatures needed to contest the elections, in the meanwhile mass-distributing his political manifesto, "{{ill|Sacred dogs|pl|Święte psy}}".<ref name=Dudek/>

