==Aftermath==
[[File:Jan Krzysztof Bielecki 1991 (cropped4).jpg|thumb|Bielecki in 1991]]
Prime Minister Mazowiecki, utterly humiliated, resigned from his office soon after the election. His successor, [[Jan Krzysztof Bielecki]], was another liberal, who was again selected by the criteria Wałęsa set out for any possible prime ministers - to be younger and less experienced politically. Bielecki, though not significantly changing the course of the country, was more subservient to the now-president and his vision.<ref name=Dudek/>

ROAD and FPD were devastated by Mazowiecki's loss. In May 1991, they united into the [[Democratic Union (Poland)|Democratic Union]] (UD) (with a small social-democratic splinter forming the [[Democratic-Social Movement (Poland)|Democratic-Social Movement]]). UD contested the [[1991 Polish parliamentary election|1991 parliamentary election]], becoming the biggest party in the Sejm with 62 seats until a splinter led by former FPD members.<ref name=Szyc>{{cite book |last=Dehnel-Szyc |first=Małgorzata |date=1991 |title=GRY Polityczne Orientacje na Dziś |trans-title=Political Moves Orientations for Today |url=https://owvolumen.pl/ksiegarnia/gry-polityczne-orientacje-na-dzis/ |language=Polish |isbn=83-85218-19-X}}</ref>

President-elect Wałęsa resigned from his role as Chairman of the Solidarity trade union shortly after becoming president, endorsing Lech Kaczyński to become the new chairman of the trade union. He faced the liberal-aligned [[Bogdan Borusewicz]] and relatively nonpartisan [[Marian Krzaklewski]]. On the 3rd Congress of Solidarity on 22–25 February, despite Wałesa's endorsement, Kaczyński lost the vote for union leadership to Krzaklewski, who would become an important politician for the duration of the next decade.<ref name=Dudek/>

Starting in January 1991,<ref name=Szyc/> Roman Bartoszcze's position within PSL began dissipating. His period as chairman concluded when after controversially signing a cooperation agreement with [[Rural Solidarity]] and [[Christian-Peasant Party|PSL "Solidarity"]], party leadership decided to oust him and replace him with [[Waldemar Pawlak]], who would become a future prime minister. Bartoszcze's ousting led to him creating a new party, the [[Polish People's Christian Forum "Fatherland"]], which had significantly less success than PSL and never won any seats in any election.<ref name=Dudek/>

Leszek Moczulski, who used the presidential election to spread his agenda and promulgate his party, succeeded in his goals, as KPN won 7.5% of the vote in the following 1991 parliamentary election, three times as much as he had personally amassed in the presidential election. However, the low result itself was a point of disappointment for Moczulski and his party.<ref name=Dudek/>

Tymiński became largely irrelevant following the election, sometimes appearing in elections as a [[perennial candidate]]. He founded [[Party X|a political party]], which was vehemently opposed by a large sector of society - 38% supported its delegalization.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.cbos.pl/SPISKOM.POL/1991/K_094_91.PDF |title=Opinie o delegalizacji list wyborczych Partii "X"}}</ref> In 1991, its candidates were disqualified in 90% of districts,<ref>{{cite book |title=Pierwsze demokratyczne wybory parlamentarne 27 X 1991 |language=pl |location=Białystok |year=2016 |first=Marek |last=Kietliński |isbn=978-83-7657-232-1 |publisher=Wydawnictwo Prymat |pages=33–34}}</ref> and would only secure 52,735 votes, winning 3 seats. The party would not win any seats in any following elections. After running for president again in [[2005 Polish presidential election|2005]], he only won 23,545 votes.<ref name=Dudek/> He contested another election in 2023, running for Senate, winning only 18,052<ref>{{cite web |url=https://sejmsenat2023.pkw.gov.pl/sejmsenat2023/pl/senat/kandydat/29622/71/2741529 |title=Wyniki głosowania na TYMIŃSKI Stanisław}}</ref> votes.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://wiadomosci.onet.pl/slask/kiedys-rzucil-wyzwanie-lechowi-walesie-dzis-chce-zostac-senatorem/p97652x |title=Kiedyś rzucił wyzwanie Lechowi Wałęsie. Dziś chce zostać senatorem |date=11 September 2023 |publisher=Onet |access-date=30 April 2024}}</ref> Though his political career was barely relevant following the first direct presidential election in Poland, his name became famous worldwide, and many would try to draw lines between Tymiński and other "populists", such as American presidential candidate [[Donald Trump]] or [[Law and Justice]] chairman Jarosław Kaczyński.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://fpif.org/welcome-birthplace-trumpism/ |title=Welcome to Eastern Europe, the Birthplace of Trumpism |last=Feffer |first=John |date=6 December 2017 |access-date=30 April 2024}}</ref>

